#+ Viewpoint descriptions for Tourist Jobs photo album
#@{
	
	key[]: "at_cut_grossglockner_desc"
	val[]: "Grossglockner, Austria’s highest peak at 3,798 metres, towers above the Alps. It is famed for its 48-km High Alpine Road with 36 hairpin turns and the Pasterze, the longest glacier in Austria and the Eastern Alps.<br><br>Saharan sand blown thousands of kilometres north sometimes turns the alpine road's snow bright yellow or orange."

	key[]: "at_cut_schonbrunn_desc"
	val[]: "Schönbrunn Palace, a former Habsburg summer residence, is a UNESCO World Heritage site and one of Europe’s finest Baroque complexes.<br><br>Its zoo, founded in 1752, is the world’s oldest continuously operating zoo."

	key[]: "at_cut_worthersee_desc"
	val[]: "Lake Wörthersee is a turquoise gem shaped by the last Ice Age, renowned for its clear, mineral-rich waters.<br><br>Legend holds an entire town and its church sank beneath the lake, and locals still claim to hear its bells ringing underwater."

	key[]: "de_cut_ettal_desc"
	val[]: "Ettal Abbey is a Benedictine monastery in the Bavarian Alps founded in 1330 by Emperor Ludwig IV of Bavaria.<br><br>Legend says Ludwig's horse knelt three times on the spot, which he took as a sign to build the monastery there."

	key[]: "de_cut_stuttgart_city_desc"
	val[]: "Stuttgart's Staatstheater, designed by architect Max Littmann, opened in 1912 as the royal court theatre of the Kingdom of Württemberg.<br><br>Stuttgart sits atop Europe's second-largest mineral water deposit, with some springs so naturally carbonated that bathing in them feels like soaking in champagne."

	key[]: "de_cut_nurnberg_city_desc"
	val[]: "Nuremberg was one of the most important cities of the Holy Roman Empire and is still famous for its remarkably extensive medieval fortifications.<br><br>In 1561 terrified Nuremberg residents witnessed a mass dawn 'aerial battle' of glowing orbs and crosses, recorded in a woodcut now cited as an early UFO sighting."

	key[]: "de_cut_frankfurt_city_desc"
	val[]: "Frankfurt's Bankenviertel financial district, nicknamed \"Mainhattan,\" hosts the headquarters of many important banks.<br><br>Frankfurt is home to one of the world's oldest trade fairs, dating back to the 12th century. Today hosts the world's largest book fair every autumn."

	key[]: "de_cut_rostock_desc"
	val[]: "Rostock's Neuer Markt square, ringed by Brick Gothic gabled merchant houses, has been the city's central marketplace since the 13th century.<br><br>Rostock is home to the oldest university on the Baltic coast, founded in 1419, more than 600 years ago."

	key[]: "de_cut_duisburg_desc"
	val[]: "Duisburg's Rathaus town hall, completed in 1902 in an Early Renaissance historicist style, stands on ground once occupied by a medieval royal palace.<br><br>Duisburg sits where the Rhine and Ruhr rivers meet, making its inland harbour the largest river port in the world."

	key[]: "de_cut_kassel_desc"
	val[]: "The first historical mentions of Kassel date back to 913, describing a fortified place by a bridge over the river.<br><br>The name itself is much older, derived from the Germanic tribe of the Chatti."

	key[]: "de_cut_berlin_desc"
	val[]: "Berlin's Victory Column, completed in 1873, commemorates nineteenth-century Prussian military victories and stands in the Tiergarten park.<br><br>The column was relocated 1.5 kilometres to its current site in 1938-39 as part of a big city redesign, gaining a fourth section that made it even taller."

	key[]: "de_cut_dresden_desc"
	val[]: "Dresden's Baroque Frauenkirche, completed in 1743 on the Neumarkt near the Elbe River, is one of the architectural landmarks of a city nicknamed \"Florence on the Elbe\" or \"German Florence\".<br><br>In 2009 Dresden became the first site in Europe stripped of UNESCO World Heritage status, losing it over the construction of a single bridge."

	key[]: "ch_cut_geneve_desc"
	val[]: "Geneva's Jet d'Eau fountain shoots lake water up to 140 metres into the air near where the Rhône exits Lake Geneva.<br><br>Since 1818, Geneva has marked the arrival of spring by watching an official horse-chestnut tree. A government official announces the season when its first leaf opens - a tradition still observed today."

	key[]: "ch_cut_zurichw_desc"
	val[]: "Zurich West is a former industrial quarter transformed since the 1990s into a trendy district anchored by the glass Prime Tower.<br><br>Zurich West's edgiest shop is built from 19 rusty shipping containers stacked nine storeys high on a former industrial wasteland."

	key[]: "ch_cut_furka_desc"
	val[]: "The Furka Pass is a high mountain pass at 2,429 metres connecting the cantons of Uri and Valais in central Switzerland.<br><br>Its hairpin bends starred in the 1964 film Goldfinger's car chase, and one curve is nicknamed \"James Bond Strasse.\""

	key[]: "nl_cut_devlieter_desc"
	val[]: "De Vlieter marks a section of the Afsluitdijk, the 32-kilometre dike that closed off the Zuiderzee to form the IJsselmeer.<br><br>The tidal channel and surrounding Wadden Sea waters near De Vlieter have swallowed an estimated 500 to 1,000 ships throughout history."

	key[]: "nl_cut_eindhoven_desc"
	val[]: "Eindhoven is a Dutch city in North Brabant known as the birthplace and longtime headquarters of electronics giant Philips.<br><br>Philips began there in 1891 making light bulbs, earning Eindhoven its nickname the \"City of Light.\""

	key[]: "nl_cut_groningen_desc"
	val[]: "Groningen is a lively university city in the northern Netherlands, dominated by the towering Martinitoren church spire.<br><br>Locals affectionately call the Martinitoren \"d’Olle Grieze,\" or \"the Old Grey One\"; it is the third tower on the site, after two predecessors were lost following lightning strikes."

	key[]: "be_cut_ardennes_desc"
	val[]: "La Roche-en-Ardenne is a small Belgian town dominated by the ruins of a medieval castle above a bend in the Ourthe river."

	key[]: "be_cut_antwerp_desc"
	val[]: "Antwerp is Belgium's second-largest city and a historic port renowned as the world's diamond-trading capital since 1447.<br><br>Roughly 80 to 90 per cent of the world's rough diamonds pass through Antwerp's Diamond Quarter each year."

	key[]: "fr_cut_brest_desc"
	val[]: "Brest’s marina lies within the historic harbour that Cardinal Richelieu transformed into a French naval base in 1631.<br><br>Brest’s marinas are a major hub for ocean racing, where elite sailors gather before setting out across the Atlantic or attempting some of sailing’s toughest world records."

	key[]: "fr_cut_marseille_desc"
	val[]: "The Old Port of Marseille has served as the city's natural harbour since Greek settlers founded Massalia around 600 BC.<br><br>It is one of the oldest continuously operating ports in Europe."

	key[]: "fr_cut_peyrat_desc"
	val[]: "Peyrat-le-Château is a small village in the Haute-Vienne department, built beside a pond on the Plateau de Millevaches in Limousin.<br><br>The Plateau de Millevaches gets its unusual name either from 'a thousand cows' that once grazed there, or from an old word for the many natural springs dotting the plateau, and nobody's quite sure which!"

	key[]: "fr_cut_rousse_desc"
	val[]: "L'Île-Rousse is a coastal town in the Balagne region of northwestern Corsica, founded in 1758 by Pasquale Paoli.<br><br>Paoli built the port so Corsicans could trade without relying on the rival Genoese-controlled harbour at Calvi."

	key[]: "fr_cut_serre_desc"
	val[]: "Lake Serre-Ponçon is a vast reservoir in the Hautes-Alpes created between 1955 and 1960 by damming the Durance river.<br><br>Lake Serre-Ponçon is so big, covering nearly 3,000 hectares, that it's nicknamed the \"sea in the mountains\", and a single lonely chapel on a tiny island still pokes just above the reservoir’s water level."

	key[]: "fr_cut_usse_desc"
	val[]: "Château d'Ussé is a fairytale-style castle in the Loire Valley, built between the 15th and 17th centuries atop a former Viking fortress.<br><br>Writer Charles Perrault reportedly drew inspiration from its white turreted towers when writing Sleeping Beauty in the 17th century."

	key[]: "it_cut_bari_desc"
	val[]: "The Port of Bari sits on Italy's Adriatic coast and has long served as a gateway between Europe and the Balkans.<br><br>Between 847 and 871 AD, Bari was the capital of its own short-lived Islamic emirate, the only such state established on mainland Italy."

	key[]: "it_cut_etna_desc"
	val[]: "Mount Etna, on Sicily's east coast, is Europe’s highest active volcano and one of the most active volcanoes on Earth.<br><br>Its name is commonly traced to the Greek Aitnē, from the word aithō, meaning \"I burn.\""

	key[]: "it_cut_neapol_desc"
	val[]: "The Port of Naples, founded amid the ancient Greek settlement of Neapolis, is one of Italy's oldest and busiest harbours.<br><br>While digging a metro station beside today’s Port of Naples, archaeologists uncovered the ancient harbour and seven shipwrecks dating from the Hellenistic and Roman periods."

	key[]: "it_cut_sassari_desc"
	val[]: "The Church of Santa Maria di Betlem in Sassari, Sardinia, is a Franciscan complex whose origins date to the 12th century.<br><br>Each August, Sassari’s guilds carry towering wooden \"candles\" weighing hundreds of kilograms to the church during the Candelieri festival, renewing an ancient vow linked to the plague."

	key[]: "it_cut_taranto_desc"
	val[]: "Taranto is a southern Italian city on the Ionian Sea, founded around 706 BC by Spartan colonists as the Greek city of Taras.<br><br>Taranto’s industrial gulf is home to a thriving dolphin population - including striped dolphins with genetic traits found nowhere else in the Mediterranean."

	key[]: "it_cut_villa_san_giovanni_desc"
	val[]: "Villa San Giovanni is a Calabrian town on mainland Italy and the main ferry terminal across the Strait of Messina to Sicily.<br><br>Villa San Giovanni was founded by silk entrepreneur Rocco Antonio Caracciolo, who united five rival hamlets. King Ferdinand IV of Naples officially named the new town in 1791."

	key[]: "balt_cut_castles_desc"
	val[]: "Hermann Castle was founded by the Danes in the 13th century and later rebuilt by the Livonian Order. Ivangorod Fortress was founded by Ivan III in 1492 to defend Muscovy’s western frontier."

	key[]: "balt_cut_convent_desc"
	val[]: "Founded in 1447 north of Pskov by Saint Euphrosynus, it grew into a noted centre of education."

	key[]: "balt_cut_daugavpils_desc"
	val[]: "Latvia's second-largest city grew from Dinaburg Castle, founded on the Daugava River in 1275 by the Livonian Order.<br><br>The city has borne at least four different names, including Dinaburg, Borisoglebsk, and Dvinsk, before becoming Daugavpils in 1920."

	key[]: "balt_cut_klaipeda_desc"
	val[]: "Klaipėda, Lithuania's only major seaport, was founded in the 1250s by the Livonian Order, a branch of the Teutonic Knights.<br><br>For about 500 years the city was never ruled by a Lithuanian state, only becoming part of Lithuania after a 1923 revolt."

	key[]: "balt_cut_riga_power_plant_desc"
	val[]: "Built on the Daugava River between 1966 and 1975, its dam created a reservoir by flooding half of the island Doles sala.<br><br>The top of the dam doubles as a public highway, and a construction-era railway that once crossed it was later dismantled."

	key[]: "balt_cut_valmiera_desc"
	val[]: "The Vidzeme Olympic Centre in Valmiera is a multifunctional hall of nearly 2,900 square metres used for basketball and floorball.<br><br>Valmiera-born racewalker Jānis Daliņš won independent Latvia’s first Summer Olympic medal, a silver in the 50-kilometre walk at the 1932 Los Angeles Games, and the city’s stadium now bears his name."

	key[]: "blke_cut_cerna_voda_desc"
	val[]: "The Anghel Saligny Bridge at Cernavodă, completed in 1895, was once the longest bridge in Europe at over four kilometres.<br><br>Retreating Romanian troops damaged the Danube bridge and destroyed its Borcea section in 1916. Both were rebuilt and a new crossing replaced them in 1987."

	key[]: "blke_cut_edirne_desc"
	val[]: "Edirne served as the capital of the Ottoman Empire for nearly a century before Constantinople was conquered in 1453.<br><br>Edirne's Kirkpinar oil-wrestling festival, held since the 14th century, drenches wrestlers in nearly two tonnes of olive oil and holds the Guinness record for oldest sporting competition."

	key[]: "blke_cut_orsova_desc"
	val[]: "Orșova is a Danube river town in southwestern Romania situated just above the Iron Gates gorge, near where the Cerna River meets the Danube.<br><br>Construction of the Iron Gates I dam raised the Danube by roughly 30 metres, submerging old Orșova in 1970. Its residents were relocated to an entirely new town constructed on higher ground between 1966 and 1971."

	key[]: "blke_cut_pernik_desc"
	val[]: "Pernik, a coal-mining city near Sofia, has hosted a major steel plant since 1953.<br><br>The mill makes most of its steel from recycled scrap, making it one of the region's most circular-economy plants."

	key[]: "blke_cut_poiana_viaduct_desc"
	val[]: "The Poiana Teiului viaduct is a 634-metre road bridge crossing the tail of Bicaz Lake in Romania's Neamț County."

	key[]: "blke_cut_r73_desc"
	val[]: "Piatra Craiului is a 25-kilometre limestone ridge in Romania's Carpathians, protected as a national park since 1990.<br><br>Its name means \"The King’s Rock,\" and the park is home to several endemic invertebrate species."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_barcelona_desc"
	val[]: "Barcelona began as the Roman settlement of Barcino, whose ancient ruins still lie beneath the modern city's streets.<br><br>Seven of Barcelona's nine UNESCO World Heritage Sites are individual works by architect Antoni Gaudí, including the unfinished Sagrada Família."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_ejido_desc"
	val[]: "El Ejido, in Almería's Campo de Dalías, hosts one of the world's largest concentrations of agricultural greenhouses.<br><br>Almería's 'Sea of Plastic' greenhouses sprawl across 370 square kilometres and reflect so much sunlight that astronauts can distinguish them from orbit."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_guadiana_desc"
	val[]: "The Guadiana International Bridge is a cable-stayed span linking Ayamonte, Spain, and Castro Marim, Portugal, at the border's southernmost crossing.<br><br>Crossing the bridge also crosses a time zone border, so travellers from Spain to Portugal can experience two separate New Year's countdowns."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_lisboa_desc"
	val[]: "The 25 de Abril Bridge spans the Tagus River connecting Lisbon and Almada and ranks among Europe's longest suspension bridges."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_olhao_desc"
	val[]: "Olhão is a whitewashed fishing town on Portugal's Algarve coast, opening directly onto the Ria Formosa lagoon.<br><br>Legend says that in 1808, seventeen Olhão fishermen sailed an open boat all the way to Brazil just to tell Portugal's exiled king his country was free."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_reservoir_desc"
	val[]: "The Barrios de Luna reservoir in León province was created in 1956 when a dam blocked the Luna River in a narrow mountain gorge.<br><br>Filling the reservoir submerged sixteen villages and settlements beneath waters that now stretch for about 40 kilometres through the valley."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_santander_desc"
	val[]: "The Port of Santander sits within walking distance of the city centre.<br><br>Founded by the Romans after the Cantabrian Wars in 19 BC, Santander’s sheltered port began as a mineral-export hub and grew alongside the city it helped create."

	key[]: "ibe_cut_zaragoza_desc"
	val[]: "One of the largest shopping and leisure complexes in Europe, covering over 200,000 square metres.<br><br>Built around an artificial lake, the development combines a shopping centre with restaurants, cinemas, sports facilities and recreation areas."

	key[]: "sc_cut_mabodalen_desc"
	val[]: "Måbødalen is a steep, glacier-carved valley near Eidfjord in western Norway, home to the Vøringsfossen waterfall.<br><br>With a breathtaking 145-metre free fall into the depths of Måbødalen, Vøringsfossen is Norway’s most iconic waterfall, drawing visitors to witness its thunderous power."

	key[]: "sc_cut_stockholm_desc"
	val[]: "Stockholm's coastline opens onto an archipelago of roughly 30,000 islands, islets, and skerries stretching into the Baltic Sea.<br><br>Stockholm's unpainted Silverpilen subway cars, introduced in 1965, inspired an urban legend of a ghost train that appears at midnight and makes any passenger who boards it vanish."

	key[]: "sc_cut_kalmar_desc"
	val[]: "Kalmar is a historic port city in southeastern Sweden, first recorded in the 11th century and home to a Renaissance castle.<br><br>Kalmar Castle has withstood 22 sieges throughout its history without ever being taken by storm."

	key[]: "sc_cut_copenhagen_desc"
	val[]: "Copenhagen is Denmark's capital, built around a historic harbour lined with canals, spires, and pedestrian streets.<br><br>Copenhagen’s Tivoli Gardens is home to Europe’s oldest operating roller coaster, the Rutschebanen, which has been thrilling riders since 1914."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_durdevica_desc"
	val[]: "Durmitor National Park in Montenegro, a UNESCO World Heritage Site known for its rugged mountains, glacial lakes and the Tara River Canyon, is home to this five-arched concrete bridge, completed in 1940 and rising more than 150 metres above the river.<br><br>Today it's one of the most popular ziplining spots in the Balkans."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_durres_desc"
	val[]: "Durrës, founded as the Greek colony Epidamnus in 627 BC, is Albania's oldest and busiest seaport.<br><br>The Romans made it the western terminus of the Via Egnatia, their great road stretching all the way to Thessaloniki."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_pristina_desc"
	val[]: "Pristina, Kosovo's capital, lies near the ruins of ancient Ulpiana and has been inhabited since Neolithic times.<br><br>By the 13th century, Pristina was an important royal and commercial centre within the medieval Serbian state, before Ottoman rule gradually took hold after 1389."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_split_desc"
	val[]: "Split’s waterfront is dominated by Diocletian’s Palace and the bell tower of St Domnius Cathedral. The entire old city grew up inside the palace walls.<br><br>Roman emperor Diocletian retired to Split and took up cabbage farming so seriously he refused pleas to reclaim the throne, saying his vegetables mattered more."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_bihac_desc"
	val[]: "Bihać’s historic centre features St Anthony’s surviving bell tower, the Captain’s Tower and the stone Turbe mausoleum. Captain’s Tower later served as an Austro-Hungarian prison. The church was largely destroyed by Allied bombing in 1944.<br><br>All three stand just metres apart, reflecting Bihać’s Christian, Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian past."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_sarajevo_desc"
	val[]: "It is the oldest and most historically significant part of Sarajevo. At its heart is the Baščaršija, Sarajevo's Ottoman-era bazaar quarter, founded in 1462 by governor Isa-beg Isaković.<br><br>Sarajevo City Hall, known as Vijećnica, is a landmark Austro-Hungarian-era building, designed in neo-Moorish style by Czech architect Karel Pařík."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_ljubljana_desc"
	val[]: "Ljubljana, Slovenia's capital, grew up along the Ljubljanica River on the site of the former Roman settlement of Emona.<br><br>Legend claims Jason and the Argonauts slew a dragon on the exact spot where Ljubljana now stands, and the beast still reigns on the city's coat of arms."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_tirana_desc"
	val[]: "Tirana was founded in 1614 by Ottoman lord Sulejman Bargjini. It became Albania's provisional capital in 1920 and its permanent capital in 1925.<br><br>Kthesa e Kamzës, one of Tirana’s major western transport gateways, links the capital with Kamëz and routes heading north and west."

	key[]: "blkw_cut_prokuplje_desc"
	val[]: "Prokuplje, seat of southern Serbia's Toplica District, traces its origins to the Roman-era settlement of Hammeum.<br><br>The town lent its name to Prokupac, a red wine grape that has been cultivated locally since medieval times."

	key[]: "gr_cut_piraeus_desc"
	val[]: "Piraeus is the main port of Athens and one of the largest passenger ports in the Mediterranean.<br><br>The Silo building was originally built for storing grain. Today it is being repurposed as the Museum of Underwater Antiquities, which will display archaeological finds recovered from Greek seas."

	key[]: "gr_cut_kavala_desc"
	val[]: "Kavala is a historic port city in northern Greece, founded in the late 7th century BC as Neapolis.<br><br>The hill has been fortified since at least the 5th century BC. The present Kavala Fortress is mainly Ottoman, rebuilt in 1425 over earlier Byzantine defences."

	key[]: "gr_cut_kalabaka_desc"
	val[]: "Meteora is a UNESCO World Heritage cluster of monasteries perched atop sandstone rock pillars near Kalabaka.<br><br>For centuries, monks hauled visitors up 400-metre cliffs in a swinging rope net, joking that it was only replaced whenever it broke."

	key[]: "gr_cut_beach_desc"
	val[]: "Faliraki is a seaside resort on Rhodes' northeastern coast, about 14 kilometres south of Rhodes Town, known for its long sandy beach.<br><br>Faliraki is one of the most popular resorts on Rhodes for water sports, from jet-skiing to parasailing high above the bay."

	key[]: "gr_cut_lixouri_desc"
	val[]: "Lixouri is Kefalonia's second-largest town, built on the Paliki Peninsula near the site of the ancient city of Pale."

	key[]: "gr_cut_corinth_desc"
	val[]: "The Corinth Canal cuts through the Isthmus of Corinth, linking the Aegean Sea and the Gulf of Corinth across a 6.4-kilometre channel.<br><br>Roman emperor Nero personally broke ground on the canal in 67 AD, nearly 1,800 years before it was finally completed in 1893."

	key[]: "gr_cut_souda_desc"
	val[]: "Souda Bay near Chania on Crete is one of the largest and deepest natural harbours in the Mediterranean.<br><br>At the entrance to the bay, the Venetians fortified the small islet of Souda in the sixteenth century; its fortress held out against Ottoman forces and remained Venetian until 1715, decades after the rest of Crete fell in 1669."

	key[]: "gr_cut_polyfytos_desc"
	val[]: "The Polyfytos Bridge spans the artificial Lake Polyfytos on the Aliakmon River, Greece's longest river, near Kozani.<br><br>Completed in 1975, the 1,372-metre bridge was designed by renowned Italian engineer Riccardo Morandi."

	key[]: "poc_cut_alesund_desc"
	val[]: "Ålesund is a major Norwegian cruise-ship port town.<br><br>German Kaiser Wilhelm II, who had previously visited the town, sent naval ships carrying building materials to help rebuild it after a devastating fire destroyed almost the entire wooden town in 1904."

	key[]: "poc_cut_bodo_desc"
	val[]: "Bodø is a coastal city just north of the Arctic Circle that was named a European Capital of Culture in 2024."

	key[]: "poc_cut_nordkapp_desc"
	val[]: "North Cape is a 307-metre cliff plateau on Magerøya island, popularly marketed as continental Europe's northernmost point.<br><br>North Cape isn't actually the northernmost point of Europe - the nearby headland Knivskjellodden extends about 1,450 metres further north."

	key[]: "poc_cut_santa_desc"
	val[]: "Santa Claus Village sits exactly on the Arctic Circle about eight kilometres north of Rovaniemi and stays open year-round.<br><br>Visitors can stand on a painted line marking the Arctic Circle and receive an official certificate proving they crossed it."

	key[]: "poc_cut_muotkatakka_desc"
	val[]: "Muotkatakka, about 12 kilometres south of Kilpisjärvi, is the highest point on Finland's entire public road network at 565.8 metres.<br><br>The road over this pass connects Finland to Norway, and reindeer are a common sight wandering right along the roadside near the summit."

	key[]: "poc_cut_farm_desc"
	val[]: "Tohmajärvi is a small municipality of forested hills and lakes in North Karelia, near Finland's eastern border with Russia.<br><br>Its coat of arms depicts an anvil and flame, commemorating the region's once-important iron industry."

	key[]: "poc_cut_viitasaari_desc"
	val[]: "Viitasaari is a lake-dotted town in Central Finland whose municipal area contains some 230 lakes.<br><br>Viitasaari's summer music festival kicks off every year with a parade of tractors rolling through town ahead of the concerts."

	key[]: "poc_cut_lalby_desc"
	val[]: "Lålby is a small hamlet within Kristinestad in the Ostrobothnia region on Finland's western coast."

	key[]: "poc_cut_kiruna_desc"
	val[]: "Kiruna is Sweden's northernmost town, built around Kirunavaara, the world's largest underground iron ore mine.<br><br>The iron ore body erupted from two kilometres underground roughly 1.6 billion years ago, and today the mine supplies about 80 per cent of the EU's iron ore."

	key[]: "poc_cut_gavle_desc"
	val[]: "Gävle is a port city on Sweden's eastern coast at the mouth of the Gavleån river in Gästrikland.<br><br>Every Advent since 1966 the city has erected a giant straw Yule goat that arsonists have burned down over 30 times."

	key[]: "poc_cut_lulea_desc"
	val[]: "Luleå, capital of Norrbotten county, sits on the Gulf of Bothnia and includes an archipelago of over 1,300 islands.<br><br>A big tech company built a giant data centre in Luleå specifically because the brutal Arctic cold works as free, natural air conditioning for its servers."

	key[]: "poc_cut_heia_desc"
	val[]: "Scenic rest stop on the E6 highway in Balsfjord, Troms, offering mountain views at about 240 metres elevation."

	key[]: "poc_cut_svolvaer_desc"
	val[]: "Svolvær, on the island of Austvågøya, is the largest town and unofficial capital of Norway's Lofoten archipelago."

	key[]: "poc_cut_bjornfjell_desc"
	val[]: "Bjørnfjell is a mountain station on the Ofoten Railway, sitting right at the Norway–Sweden border near Narvik.<br><br>Because it sits so high in the mountains, Bjørnfjell station is often blanketed in snow for much of the year, and the railway line here is considered one of the most scenic train rides in Northern Europe."

	key[]: "poc_cut_trondheim_desc"
	val[]: "Trondheim, founded around 997 as Kaupangr, later known as Nidaros, served as Norway's capital during the Viking Age.<br><br>Its Nidaros Cathedral, built over the grave of St. Olav, is the world's northernmost medieval Gothic cathedral."

	key[]: "is_cut_thingvellir_desc"
	val[]: "Þingvellir National Park lies in a rift valley between the North American and Eurasian tectonic plates. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2004.<br><br>Iceland's parliament, the Alþingi, first met here in the open air in 930, making it one of the oldest parliaments in the world."

	key[]: "is_cut_barthastran_desc"
	val[]: "Barðastrandarvegur (Route 62) follows the Barðaströnd coast in the southern Westfjords, along the northern shore of the wide Breiðafjörður bay.<br><br>The Viking Hrafna-Flóki Vilgerðarson spent a winter on this coast, and after seeing a fjord full of drift ice from a nearby mountain, he gave the whole country its name: Iceland."

	key[]: "is_cut_lagoon_desc"
	val[]: "This geothermal lagoon spa sits in a lava field on the Reykjanes Peninsula, where bathers relax in milky-blue water averaging 37–39°C.<br><br>The lagoon is man-made: the spa was founded to hatness warm runoff water from the nearby geothermal power station which opened in 1976."

	key[]: "is_cut_selfoss_desc"
	val[]: "Selfoss is the largest town in South Iceland. It was built on the banks of the Ölfusá river which got its first suspension bridge in 1891.<br><br>Since 2011, Selfoss has been among the places that host Iceland's Strongest Man competition, where giants of strength lift boulders and pull heavy trucks."

	key[]: "is_cut_seljaland_desc"
	val[]: "Seljalandsfoss is a 60-metre waterfall in South Iceland, fed by a river that starts at the Eyjafjallajökull glacier volcano.<br><br>A footpath leads right behind the falling water into a small cave, so visitors can watch the waterfall from the inside."

	key[]: "is_cut_geyser_desc"
	val[]: "Strokkur, located in the Haukadalur Geothermal Field, is Iceland's most active geyser, erupting every few minutes.<br><br>Its neighbour, the Great Geysir, gave its name to every geyser in the world; it comes from the Icelandic verb geysa, meaning \"to go quickly forward\"."

	key[]: "is_cut_gullfoss_desc"
	val[]: "Gullfoss, aka \"Golden Falls\" is a waterfall located on the Hvítá river and it tumbles in two steps of 11 and 21 metres into a crevice 32 metres deep.<br><br>When foreign investors wanted to use the waterfall for electricity, local farmer's daughter Sigríður Tómasdóttir campaigned to protect it. Above the waterfall, there is a stone memorial honoring her."

	key[]: "is_cut_joekul_desc"
	val[]: "Jökulsárlón is a glacier lagoon at the head of Breiðamerkurjökull, an outlet glacier of the vast Vatnajökull ice cap.<br><br>The lagoon only began forming in the 1930s as the glacier retreated, and at over 280 metres it is now the deepest lake in Iceland."

	key[]: "is_cut_gothafoss_desc"
	val[]: "Goðafoss is a 12-metre-high, 30-metre-wide waterfall on the Skjálfandafljót river in North Iceland.<br><br>Its name is usually translated as \"waterfall of the gods\", though it may come from two rocks at the falls that look like pagan idols."

	key[]: "is_cut_canyon_desc"
	val[]: "Stuðlagil is a canyon in East Iceland's Jökuldalur valley, lined with tall hexagonal basalt columns along the Jökla river.<br><br>The canyon was hidden underwater until the Kárahnjúkar dam, finished in 2009, lowered the river, and it only became famous after a photographer shared it in 2016."
#@}

